-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">32</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q32\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>around 650 BC, Babylonians started forecasting, using weather phenomena such as <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">33</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q33\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>by 300 BC, the Chinese had a calendar made up of a number of <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">34</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q34\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span>&nbsp;connected with the weather</li></ul><p><strong>Ancient Greeks</strong></p><ul><li>a more scientific approach</li><li>Aristotle tried to explain the formation of various weather phenomena</li><li>Aristotle also described haloes and <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">35</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q35\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li></ul><p><strong>Middle Ages</strong></p><ul><li>Aristotle’s work considered accurate</li><li>many proverbs, e.g. about the significance of the colour of the <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">36</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q36\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span>, passed on accurate information.</li></ul><p><strong>15th-19th centuries</strong></p><ul><li>15th century: scientists recognised value of <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">37</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q37\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span>&nbsp;for the first time</li><li>Galileo invented the <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">38</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q38\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>Pascal showed relationship between atmospheric pressure and altitude</li><li>from the 17th century, scientists could measure atmospheric pressure and temperature</li><li>18th century: Franklin identified the movement of <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">39</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q39\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li><li>19th century: data from different locations could be sent to the same place by <span class=\"source-question-item\"><strong class=\"source-question-number\">40</strong><span data-luma-question=\"en-c14-t2-l-q40\" data-luma-kind=\"text\"></span></span></li></ul></div></div><div class=\"source-questions\"><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div><div class=\"source-question-item\"></div></div>","sourceQuestionCount":10}],"audio_fallbacks":["https://engnovate.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/cambridge-ielts-14-academic-listening-2-audio-4.mp3"],"order_index":4}],"questions":[{"id":"en-c14-t2-l-q1","part_id":"en-c14-t2-l-p1","group_id":"en-c14-t2-l-g1-0","question_type":"source_text","ieltsQuestionType":"note_completion","question_text":"","correct_answer":"219 442 9785/2194429785","correct_answer_variants":["219 442 9785","2194429785"],"answerCount":1,"sourceNumbers":[1],"sourceInputName":"ielts_listening_answer_12595_1","order_index":1,"points":1},{"id":"en-c14-t2-l-q2","part_id":"en-c14-t2-l-p1","group_id":"en-c14-t2-l-g1-0","question_type":"source_text","ieltsQuestionType":"note_completion","question_text":"","correct_answer":"10 October/10th October/October 10/October 10th","correct_answer_variants":["10 October","10th October","October 10","October 10th"],"answerCount":1,"sourceNumbers":[2],"sourc